## 12: Make me a simple 4-week beginner 10k plan around my running days.
[completed 8s] Here's a simple 4-week plan built around your Tuesday / Thursday / Sunday rhythm — starting next Tuesday (10 Nov), so it finishes in early December. It's run/walk-based at first, which is exactly how beginners should build to 10k safely. If 10k in March is the goal, you can repeat or extend this block afterwards with longer Sunday runs.

**Week 1 — Get going (10–16 Nov)**
- **Tue:** 30 min run/walk — 2 min easy run / 1 min walk, repeat ×10
- **Thu:** 25 min run/walk — same intervals
- **Sun:** 40 min — 3 min run / 1 min walk, repeat ×10

**Week 2 — Stretch the runs (17–23 Nov)**
- **Tue:** 30 min — 4 min run / 1 min walk ×6
- **Thu:** 30 min — 4 min run / 1 min walk ×6
- **Sun:** 45 min — 5 min run / 1 min walk ×7–8

**Week 3 — Time on feet (24–30 Nov)**
- **Tue:** 35 min — 6 min run / 1 min walk ×5
- **Thu:** 35 min — 6 min run / 1 min walk ×5
- **Sun:** 55 min — 8 min run / 2 min walk ×5–6

**Week 4 — First long run (1–7 Dec)**
- **Tue:** 30 min easy — 8 min run / 2 min walk ×3
- **Thu:** 25 min easy + 4 short strides (20 s relaxed fast)
- **Sun:** 10k day! Run/walk the distance — 9 min run / 2 min walk — even if it takes 70–80 min. That's your benchmark going into December.

**A few Lisbon-flavoured tips:**
- **Sundays** are your long-run day — riverside flatness along the Tejo (Alcântara → Parque das Nações) is perfect for those, and you can lengthen it gradually in the December–February blocks.
- **Tuesdays/Thursdays** can be hillier (Parque Eduardo VII, Graça/Alfama slopes) once the walk breaks disappear — free strength work.
- Keep all of it *conversational pace* for now; speed comes later.
- November Lisbon mornings are ~12–15 °C — ideal running weather, just bring a light layer.

Two rules that matter more than the plan itself: never skip the walk breaks in weeks 1–2, and if anything hurts (not just sore), take an extra rest day rather than pushing through.

