## 14: Explain compound interest with a simple example.
[completed 4s] **Compound interest** is interest that earns interest. Instead of only earning returns on your original money, your earnings get added to the pot — and next year's interest is calculated on the bigger amount.

**The simple example: €1,000 at 5% per year**

| Year | Start amount | Interest (5%) | End amount |
|------|-------------|---------------|------------|
| 1 | €1,000.00 | €50.00 | €1,050.00 |
| 2 | €1,050.00 | €52.50 | €1,102.50 |
| 3 | €1,102.50 | €55.13 | €1,157.63 |

Notice year 2: you earn €52.50, not €50, because the 5% is applied to €1,050 — your original €1,000 **plus** the €50 you already made. That extra €2.50 is compound interest at work. It looks tiny at first, but it snowballs:

- **After 10 years:** €1,000 → **€1,629** (you gained €629 — most of it in later years)
- **After 30 years:** €1,000 → **€4,321** — over 4× your money, without adding a cent

**The intuitive way to think about it:** simple interest (interest only on the original €1,000) gives you a straight line — €50/year forever. Compound interest gives you a curve that gets steeper over time, because each year's earnings join the team and start earning too.

**A real-life version, with contributions:** if you put away €100/month into something earning 5%/year, after 10 years you'd have ~€15,500 — of which only €12,000 is what you put in. The other ~€3,500 is compound growth. Stretch it to 30 years and you'd have ~€83,000 from €36,000 contributed.

**Two things worth knowing:**
- **Time matters more than the rate.** Starting 10 years earlier often beats earning 1–2% more. That's why "start early" is the whole game.
- **It cuts both ways** — debt compounds too. A credit card balance at 18% grows against you the same way, which is why paying off high-interest debt first is usually the best "investment" available.
## 15: Convert 180 g of flour, 2 cups of milk and 350°F into metric units and Celsius.
[completed 4s] Here are the conversions:

| Original | Metric / Celsius |
|----------|------------------|
| 180 g flour | **180 g** (already metric — grams are grams everywhere) |
| 2 cups milk | **≈ 480 ml** (1 US cup = 240 ml) |
| 350°F | **≈ 175 °C** (350°F − 32) × 5⁄9 = 176.7 °C, usually rounded to 175 °C in recipes |

Two small notes:

- **Cups:** if the recipe is British/Australian, a cup is 250 ml, so 2 cups = 500 ml — for milk in baking, the ~20 ml difference rarely matters.
- **Oven temp:** 175 °C is the standard equivalent; if your oven only has 10-degree steps, 180 °C works fine (just check a couple of minutes earlier).

