## 17: Give me five tips to stay focused while working from home.
[completed 4s] Here are five tips that actually work for focus at home, especially for design work:

**1. Create a "commute" ritual**
It sounds silly, but a short fake commute — a 10-minute walk around the block, a coffee made deliberately, changing out of pyjamas — signals to your brain that work has started. Without it, WFH days blur and you end up half-working for 10 hours.

**2. Timebox in blocks, not hours**
Design is deep work — you can't do it in 5-minute scraps. Try 50–90 minute focused blocks with a real break between (not "checking your phone" — an actual break: stretch, water, look out the window). A timer helps; the pomodoro-style 25/5 works if your sessions tend to stall.

**3. Separate "maker" time from "admin" time**
Batch the shallow stuff — emails, invoices, client pings, file exports — into one or two fixed slots a day (say, 11:00 and 16:00). Creative work needs long uninterrupted stretches; every context switch costs you ~15 minutes of momentum getting back in.

**4. Give your space a work mode**
Even in a small Lisbon apartment: one spot that's *the* work spot, cleared at the end of the day. Same playlist or instrumental album when you sit down can act as a focus trigger. And keep the phone in another room (or at least face-down and on Do Not Disturb) — notification-checking is the biggest focus killer there is.

**5. End the day with a 3-item shutdown note**
Before you close the laptop, write the top 3 things for tomorrow. It takes two minutes, stops the "did I forget something?" evening fog, and means you start the next morning executing instead of deciding — which is where most of the early-day focus is quietly lost.

Bonus, design-specific one: keep reference gathering and *making* separate. Opening Pinterest/Behance/Dribbble "for inspiration" mid-project is procrastination wearing a work badge — collect references in a dedicated session, then work.

And a Lisbon bonus: the afternoon light + a proper break outside does more for your 15:00 slump than a third coffee ever will. ☀️
