## 25: Give me a 10-minute stretching routine for after runs.
[completed 6s] Here's a simple 10-minute routine to do right after a run, while your muscles are still warm. Hold each stretch for about 45 seconds (30 seconds per side where one side is worked at a time), and don't bounce — steady, easy tension.

**1. Standing quad stretch (30 s per leg)**
Stand tall, pull one heel toward your glutes, knees together. Hold a wall for balance if needed.

**2. Standing hamstring stretch (30 s per leg)**
Prop one heel on a low step, hinge forward from the hips with a flat back until you feel it behind the thigh.

**3. Calf stretch (30 s per leg)**
Hands on a wall, one leg back, back heel down and leg straight. Do a second shorter hold with the back knee slightly bent to hit the soleus.

**4. Hip flexor lunge stretch (30 s per leg)**
Step into a shallow lunge, back knee down or hovering, tuck your pelvis under and gently push the hips forward. Your calves and hips take the most load on Lisbon's hills, so give these real time.

**5. Figure-4 glute stretch (30 s per side)**
Standing or seated, cross one ankle over the opposite knee and sit back into it. Great if you feel any piriformis tightness.

**6. Pigeon pose or reclined figure-4 (1 min total)**
A deeper glute/hip opener — pick whichever version your knees like.

**7. IT band / side lean (30 s per side)**
Standing, cross one leg behind the other and lean sideways away from the back leg.

**8. Chest and shoulder opener (45 s)**
Fingers interlaced behind your back, lift the chest, open the shoulders — undoes the hunched running posture.

**9. Standing forward fold with rag-doll sway (45 s)**
Feet hip-width, knees soft, hang forward and gently sway side to side to release the whole back line.

**10. Easy calf-and-breath finisher (30 s)**
Rise slowly onto your toes and lower down a few times, breathing deeply, letting the heart rate settle.

A couple of tips:
- Keep it gentle — stretching after running is about easing tension, not forcing range; you shouldn't be wincing.
- On your harder or longer days (e.g. before the March 2027 race builds up), spend extra time on calves and hip flexors and shorten the rest.

